<h1>Application integration (OIDC)</h1>
<p class="lead">Booster Auth is a standard OpenID Connect provider. Any library that supports OIDC works; this page lists the values you need and
    examples for common platforms.</p>

<h2 id="values">Values you need</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Issuer (authority)</td><td><code>https://auth.company.com/idp</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Discovery</td><td><code>https://auth.company.com/idp/.well-known/openid-configuration</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>client_id</td><td>The application's client key, e.g. <code>acme-web</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>redirect_uri</td><td>A URI registered on the application, e.g. <code>https://app.company.com/callback</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Flow</td><td>Authorization Code + PKCE (<code>S256</code> required)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scopes</td><td><code>openid profile email offline_access</code> + scopes allowed for the application</td></tr>
        <tr><td>resource (optional)</td><td>The target API (audience) of the token, e.g. <code>https://api.company.com</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout info"><p><b>One issuer.</b> The issuer is the same for all pools (<code>/idp</code>); the pool a user signs in to is determined by
    the pool the <code>client_id</code> belongs to.</p></div>

<h2 id="endpoints">Endpoints</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Address</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Authorization</td><td><code>/idp/authorize</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Token</td><td><code>/idp/token</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>JWKS (signing keys, RS256)</td><td><code>/idp/.well-known/jwks.json</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>User info</td><td><code>/idp/me</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Logout</td><td><code>/idp/logout</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="spa">SPA / JavaScript</h2>
<p>This example uses the popular <code>oidc-client-ts</code> library; no client secret lives in the browser and PKCE is applied automatically.</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">JavaScript</span><pre>import { UserManager } from "oidc-client-ts";

const auth = new UserManager({
  authority: "https://auth.company.com/idp",
  client_id: "acme-web",
  redirect_uri: "https://app.company.com/callback",
  post_logout_redirect_uri: "https://app.company.com/",
  response_type: "code",
  scope: "openid profile email offline_access",
});

// Sign in
await auth.signinRedirect();

// On the /callback page
const user = await auth.signinRedirectCallback();
console.log(user.profile.email, user.access_token);</pre></div>

<h2 id="dotnet">ASP.NET Core (server-side web)</h2>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">C#</span><pre>builder.Services
    .AddAuthentication(o =>
    {
        o.DefaultScheme = CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
        o.DefaultChallengeScheme = OpenIdConnectDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
    })
    .AddCookie()
    .AddOpenIdConnect(o =>
    {
        o.Authority = "https://auth.company.com/idp";
        o.ClientId = "acme-web";
        o.ResponseType = "code";
        o.UsePkce = true;
        o.Scope.Add("email");
        o.MapInboundClaims = false;    // keep "sub", "email", "role" as is
    });</pre></div>

<h2 id="api">Protecting your API</h2>
<p>Your API validates incoming access tokens with the issuer's JWKS keys. The token's <code>aud</code> is the API address the client requested as
    <code>resource</code>.</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">C# — Web API</span><pre>builder.Services
    .AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddJwtBearer(o =>
    {
        o.Authority = "https://auth.company.com/idp";
        o.Audience = "https://api.company.com";
        o.MapInboundClaims = false;
    });

app.MapGet("/orders", () => "...")
   .RequireAuthorization(p => p.RequireClaim("scope", "orders:read"));</pre></div>

<h2 id="tokens">Token contents</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Claims</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Access token</td><td><code>sub</code>, <code>aud</code>, <code>azp</code>, <code>scope</code>, <code>role</code> (one per role), <code>sid</code>, <code>principal_type</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>ID token</td><td><code>sub</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>email_verified</code>, <code>nonce</code>, <code>auth_time</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/idp/me</code></td><td><code>sub</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>email_verified</code>, <code>userpool</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>By default access tokens live 15 minutes and refresh tokens 14 days; change them in the pool's session policy.</p>

<h2 id="service">Server to server (service account)</h2>
<p>For services that run without a human user, create a <b>service account</b> in the pool; its key and secret are shown only once. The
    application must have the <i>Client Credentials</i> grant type enabled.</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">HTTP</span><pre>POST https://auth.company.com/idp/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials
&amp;client_id=acme-worker
&amp;service_account_key=SA_KEY
&amp;service_account_secret=SA_SECRET
&amp;resource=https://api.company.com</pre></div>
<p>The response contains only an access token; no ID token or refresh token is issued.</p>

<h2 id="logout">Logout</h2>
<p>Redirect the user to <code>/idp/logout</code>; the session is closed and the user returns to the post-logout URI registered on the
    application. OIDC libraries do this with a call like <code>signoutRedirect()</code>.</p>
